// firebase/firestore.rules.prod
//
// ============================================================================
// THIS IS THE PRODUCTION RULESET. MERGING A CHANGE TO IT DEPLOYS IT (T105).
// ============================================================================
//
// `firebase.json` points its `firestore.rules` key here, and the merge
// workflow (`firebase-hosting-merge.yml`, job `deploy_rules`) deploys it after
// the Cloud Functions and before Hosting. Do not edit the rules in the
// Firebase console: the next merge overwrites the console with this file.
//
// `firestore.rules` beside it is the permissive DEVELOPMENT ruleset -- the
// emulators run without it, from `firebase.emulators.json` -- and is
// deliberately NOT the production policy. Do not "reconcile" the two: its
// blanket `allow read, write: if true` is correct for an emulator and would be
// catastrophic in production.
//
// DEPLOY ORDER: rules go out with the functions they rely on and BEFORE the
// frontend that relies on them. A change that must follow the frontend
// instead (a rule that refuses what the live frontend still writes, as
// 2026-10-04b did) has to ship in two merges: the frontend first, the rule
// in a later one.
//
// Revisions up to 2026-10-04b were pasted into the console by hand; their
// "paste" wording below is history.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-07-29 -- READ BEFORE DEPLOYING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This is the first substantive change to this policy since it was transcribed
// from the console on 2026-07-28. Every claim below was verified by loading
// this file into the Firestore emulator under a scratch project ID and running
// a 17-check battery as a real group admin and a real plain member. The same
// battery ran against the PREVIOUS revision first, so each change is a measured
// difference rather than an assertion.
//
// FOUR CHANGES, in descending order of importance:
//
// 1. RELAXED, AS REQUESTED: a group member may now UPDATE and DELETE campaign
//    content created by any other member. Previously update required
//    `createdBy == uid || isGroupAdmin`, and delete required `isGroupAdmin`
//    outright -- so a member could not even delete an NPC they had created
//    themselves. Meanwhile the app offers Edit and Delete to everyone and has
//    no creator check anywhere in `src/`, so every such attempt failed at write
//    time (bug #1406). This is a shared campaign journal; collective
//    record-keeping is the point, and `createdBy`/`modifiedBy` still record
//    authorship separately, so the audit trail survives.
//
// 2. SECURITY FIX -- personal notes were readable by the whole group.
//    Notes live at `groups/{groupId}/users/{uid}/notes`, and the previous
//    policy had no rule for them. They were therefore matched by the catch-all
//    `match /{collection}/{docId}/{nestedCollection}/{nestedDocId}`, whose read
//    rule is `isGroupMember(groupId)` -- so ANY member of the group could read,
//    list and write ANY other member's private notes. Verified: all three were
//    ALLOWED under the previous revision. The app never exposes this (the
//    client only ever queries its own uid's path), so nothing leaked through
//    the UI -- but anyone with the Firebase SDK and a group membership could
//    read the lot. Notes are now explicitly owner-only.
//
// 3. SECURITY FIX -- a member could make themselves a group admin. FULLY CLOSED.
//    `allow create, update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId` on
//    the group profile let a member write ANY field of their own profile,
//    including `role`. One `updateDoc({role: "admin"})` and they could delete
//    the group, remove other users, and mint registration tokens. Verified
//    ALLOWED under the previous revision.
//    Now closed on BOTH paths: `update` may no longer touch `role`, and `create`
//    may only ever write `role: "member"`. The second half is possible because
//    group creation moved into the `createGroup` Cloud Function -- see change 5.
//
// 4. SECURITY FIX -- the catch-all wildcard was over-broad on create.
//    `match /{collection}/{docId}` with `allow create: if isGroupMember` let a
//    member create documents in ANY group-level collection, including
//    `registrationTokens` -- i.e. mint their own invitations. Verified ALLOWED
//    under the previous revision. The wildcard is gone; `campaigns` is now
//    matched explicitly, and anything not matched falls through to the final
//    deny. A group's collections are exactly `campaigns`, `users`, `usernames`
//    and `registrationTokens` (confirmed via `listCollectionIds` against the
//    emulator), all four of which have explicit blocks below.
//
// 5. GROUP CREATION MOVED SERVER-SIDE, which is what makes change 3 complete.
//    `GroupService.createGroup` used to run a client-side `runTransaction` that
//    wrote the group document, the caller's global user document, their group
//    profile with `role: 'admin'`, and their username reservation. Because a
//    client had to be allowed to write `role: "admin"` for that to work, the
//    `create` rule could not restrict the role at all -- which left the two-step
//    escalation open however tightly `update` was guarded.
//
//    It now runs in the `createGroup` Cloud Function (`onCall`,
//    `europe-west1`), in an Admin SDK transaction that writes the same four
//    documents with the same field shapes. The Admin SDK bypasses rules, so the
//    creator still becomes an admin while no client is ever permitted to write
//    that value.
//
//    Conditioning the rule on the group document's `createdBy` was considered
//    and rejected instead: the group doc is written in the SAME transaction as
//    the profile, so a rules `get()` may not see committed data, and getting
//    that wrong breaks group creation for everyone.
//
//    ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS. The Cloud Function must be deployed BEFORE this
//    ruleset. Deploying the rules first would reject the old client-side
//    create path and break group creation for anyone on the currently-deployed
//    frontend. Deploy functions, then the frontend, then these rules.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-09-23 -- READ BEFORE DEPLOYING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The console was re-read on 2026-09-23 before this revision and matched the
// previous revision of this file rule for rule, so the 2026-07-29 revision
// above IS live. Every change below is pinned by
// `functions/test/rules/firestore-rules-prod.test.ts`, which loads this file
// into the emulator. Each ALLOWED below was measured against the previous
// revision first.
//
// Membership and administration are now written only by Cloud Functions.
// Every hole closed here had the same shape: the rules trusted a field that
// the caller could write about themselves.
//
// 1. SECURITY FIX (T052) -- anyone could join any group without an invitation.
//    `isGroupMember` trusts `users/{uid}.groups`, and a user could write that
//    array freely. Measured: a stranger listed a group's id in their own
//    profile and could then read and write its campaigns. Group ids leak
//    through every invitation link. Clients may no longer write `groups` at
//    all; joining goes through the `redeemInvitation` Cloud Function, which is
//    also the first place a token's `used` and `expiresAt` are enforced.
//
// 2. SECURITY FIX -- any new account could make itself a GLOBAL admin.
//    `users/{userId}` create checked only the uid, so a fresh account could
//    write `isAdmin: true` into its own profile and then pass `isGlobalAdmin`.
//    Measured: allowed. Clients may no longer create that document at all;
//    `redeemInvitation` and `createGroup` create it with the Admin SDK.
//
// 3. Clients may no longer create their own group profile, reserve a username
//    in a group they are not in, or mark a token used. All three were the
//    client half of the old join; the function does them now.
//
// 4. Roles change only through the `setMemberRole` Cloud Function (T034),
//    which refuses to leave a group without an admin (T035). A rule cannot
//    count admins, so the group-admin update below may no longer touch `role`.
//
// 5. BUG FIX -- a member could not rename themselves. `changeGroupUsername`
//    deletes the old reservation, and only a global admin could delete one.
//    Measured: denied for a plain member. An owner may now delete their own.
//
// ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS, as in 2026-07-29: functions first, then the
// frontend, then these rules. Rules first would break joining for anyone on
// the frontend currently deployed, which still writes membership itself.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-04 -- T084, first half
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One addition, pinned by the "pending uploads" tests in
// `functions/test/rules/firestore-rules-prod.test.ts`:
//
// 6. NEW -- `groups/{groupId}/pendingUploads/{uploadId}`. A member records an
//    image upload before writing the file and deletes the entry once the
//    document points at it, so the daily sweep can tell an upload whose
//    document write is queued in an offline tab from an orphan (IMG-003).
//    Create is for members, in their own name, at the server's time, naming
//    a file in this group under the entry's own id; delete is owner-only.
//
// Deploy order does not matter for this one. Before it is pasted, the client
// finds its entry refused and uploads as it always has; after, the sweep
// spares pending uploads. The second half of T084 -- refusing an image path
// with no live entry, so a write that arrives after the lease cannot point at
// a deleted file -- is a later revision, and THAT one must follow the client.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REVISION 2026-10-04b -- T084, second half -- READ BEFORE DEPLOYING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One change, pinned by the "an image path needs its upload's live entry"
// tests in `functions/test/rules/firestore-rules-prod.test.ts`:
//
// 7. NEW -- a write that points a document at a different image file needs
//    that file's `pendingUploads` entry, naming exactly that path and still
//    within the 30-day lease (`imageLeased` below). It covers an NPC's or a
//    location's `image`, a campaign's `banner` and a group's `crest`. Leaving
//    the image as it is, or removing it, needs nothing. The daily sweep
//    deletes a file once its entry's lease is over, so without this a document
//    write queued for longer than that could still land and point at the
//    deleted file (IMG-003's last case).
//
//    A side effect, on purpose: a write that sends back a stale copy of the
//    whole record (T083) can no longer put back a picture that has since been
//    replaced and deleted. It is refused instead.
//
// ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS: the frontend first, then these rules. A frontend
// that writes no entry (anything before T084's first half) would have every
// image change refused. Paste this once T084's client halves are live, and
// preferably once T083 has stopped edits sending the whole record, so that a
// stale tab's unrelated edit is not refused along with the picture it carries.
//
// LIVE: the maintainer pasted this revision into the console on 2026-10-05,
// after both client halves of T084 and T083's field patches had shipped.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// KEEPING IT HONEST
// Change the production rules here, in the same PR as whatever motivated the
// change, never in the console. Every merge to `main` redeploys this file, so a
// console edit lasts only until then.
//
// Note the rules are path-scoped, so a `collectionGroup` query has no matching
// rule and WILL be denied.

rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // Basic helper functions
    function isSignedIn() {
      return request.auth != null;
    }

    function isGlobalAdmin() {
      return isSignedIn() &&
        exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(request.auth.uid)) &&
        get(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(request.auth.uid)).data.isAdmin == true;
    }

    function isGroupMember(groupId) {
      let userDoc = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(request.auth.uid));
      return isSignedIn() && userDoc != null && userDoc.data.groups != null &&
             userDoc.data.groups.hasAny([groupId]);
    }

    function isGroupAdmin(groupId) {
      let userProfileDoc = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/users/$(request.auth.uid));
      return isSignedIn() && userProfileDoc != null && userProfileDoc.data.role == "admin";
    }

    // The id of a name's reservation: the name lower-cased the way the client
    // does it (`toLowerCase`). The rules' `lower()` folds only ASCII, and
    // usernames also allow Æ, Ø and Å (`UserService.validateGroupUsername`).
    function reservationKey(username) {
      return username.lower().replace('Æ', 'æ').replace('Ø', 'ø').replace('Å', 'å');
    }

    // Whether this write may leave `field` -- an image, `StoredImage` -- as it
    // does (T084). Unchanged or removed needs nothing. Pointing at a different
    // file needs that file's upload entry, below.
    function imageLeased(groupId, field) {
      let after = request.resource.data.get(field, null);
      let before = resource == null ? null : resource.data.get(field, null);
      return after == null || after == before || uploadLeased(groupId, after.path);
    }

    // Whether `path` has a `pendingUploads` entry naming exactly it, inside the
    // 30-day lease after which the daily sweep may delete the file
    // (`PENDING_LEASE_MS` in `functions/src/imageMaintenance/sweepOrphanedImages.ts`).
    function uploadLeased(groupId, path) {
      let segments = path.split('/');
      let entry = /databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/pendingUploads/$(segments[segments.size() - 1]);
      return exists(entry) &&
             get(entry).data.path == path &&
             get(entry).data.createdAt + duration.value(30, 'd') > request.time;
    }

    // Admin collection for system-wide settings
    // Only the global admin can access this
    match /admin/{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if isGlobalAdmin();
    }

    // Global user profiles - strict email protection
    match /users/{userId} {
      // Users can ONLY read their own complete profile (including email)
      allow read: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId;

      // EVERYONE ELSE (including global admin) cannot see emails
      allow read: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid != userId &&
                   !("email" in resource.data);

      // No client create. This used to be `request.auth.uid == userId` and
      // nothing more, so a fresh account could create its profile with
      // `isAdmin: true` (2026-09-23, change 2). The profile is created by the
      // `redeemInvitation` and `createGroup` Cloud Functions.

      // Users can update only the fields the client itself writes. Every other
      // field is server-owned: `groups` is what `isGroupMember` reads, so
      // writing it was joining any group without an invitation (T052), and
      // `entityExtractionUsage` is what the quota reads, so writing it was
      // unlimited paid AI use (T080). An allowlist, so a field added later is
      // server-owned until someone decides otherwise.
      allow update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId &&
                     request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                       .hasOnly(['activeGroupId', 'lastLogin', 'preferences']);

      // Only global admin can assign admin privileges, but cannot modify emails
      allow update: if isGlobalAdmin() &&
                     !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(['email']);

      // Only global admin can delete users (but not themselves)
      allow delete: if isGlobalAdmin() && request.auth.uid != userId;
    }

    // Group-level access
    match /groups/{groupId} {
      // Group data is readable by its members and global admin
      allow read: if isGroupMember(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();

      // Anyone signed in can create a group (they become the admin)
      allow create: if isSignedIn() && imageLeased(groupId, 'crest');

      // Group admins can update, global admin can do anything
      allow update: if (isGroupAdmin(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin()) &&
                     imageLeased(groupId, 'crest');

      // Only group admins or global admin can delete a group
      allow delete: if isGroupAdmin(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();

      // Group user profiles
      match /users/{userId} {
        // Group members can see other group members' profiles
        allow read: if isGroupMember(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();

        // No client create. Every group profile is written by a Cloud
        // Function: `createGroup` for the creator (as admin), and
        // `redeemInvitation` for everyone who joins (as member). The previous
        // revision allowed a self-create as "member" for the client-side join,
        // which no longer exists (2026-09-23, change 3).

        // Users can update only the fields the client itself writes on their
        // own group profile. `role` was a one-write escalation to group admin;
        // `userId` and `id` steered an admin's removal onto another member
        // (T080). Neither is in the list, nor anything added later.
        //
        // A new `username` must be reserved to the writer once the write
        // lands -- by the same transaction, as `changeGroupUsername` does, or
        // already (a change of case). Without it a member could take another
        // member's name, and leaving would release that member's reservation.
        allow update: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId &&
                       request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                         .hasOnly(['activeCampaignId', 'activeCharacterId', 'characters', 'username']) &&
                       (!request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(['username']) ||
                        getAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/groups/$(groupId)/usernames/$(reservationKey(request.resource.data.username))).data.userId == request.auth.uid);

        // The global admin can update other profiles, but not their role (the
        // `setMemberRole` Cloud Function, which refuses to leave a group with
        // no admin -- T034, T035), identity or name. Group admins edit no
        // other member's profile from the client; nothing in the app does.
        allow update: if isGlobalAdmin() &&
                       !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                         .hasAny(['role', 'userId', 'id', 'username']);

        // No client delete. Leaving and removal both run in the
        // `removeUserFromGroup` Cloud Function, which also takes the group off
        // `users/{uid}.groups`. A raw delete left that array alone, so the
        // member vanished from the roster and kept their access (T080).

        // Personal notes -- PRIVATE TO THEIR AUTHOR.
        // This block is new. Without it these documents fell through to the old
        // catch-all wildcard, which made every member's notes readable, listable
        // and writable by the entire group. The app's design has always treated
        // notes as personal (the client only ever reads its own uid's path);
        // this makes the rules agree with that design.
        //
        // Deliberately not readable by group admins or the global admin either:
        // "private" that an admin can read is not private. If a future feature
        // lets a user SHARE a note, it should copy or publish it into a
        // campaign-scoped collection rather than widening this rule.
        match /notes/{noteId} {
          allow read, write: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId;
        }

        // A reader's place in each campaign's story, one document per campaign
        // (T073). Private for the same reason notes are: it records what one
        // player has read. It used to be one campaign-wide document every
        // member overwrote. Writing also requires membership, which the notes
        // rule does not: an owner-only check alone would let an account with
        // no invitation write into any group's subtree under its own uid.
        match /story-progress/{campaignId} {
          allow read: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId;
          allow write: if isSignedIn() && request.auth.uid == userId && isGroupMember(groupId);
        }
      }

      // Username reservations in group
      match /usernames/{username} {
        // Allow username availability check for all (needed for registration)
        allow get: if true;

        // A member may reserve a name for themselves in their own group -- a
        // rename. Joining reserves the first one server-side, so a non-member
        // no longer needs to, and could previously squat names in any group.
        allow create: if isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                       request.resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;

        // Group members and global admin can list all usernames
        allow list: if isGroupMember(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();

        // A member may release their own reservation -- the other half of a
        // rename. Before 2026-09-23 only the global admin could, so every
        // rename by anyone else was denied (change 5).
        allow delete: if isSignedIn() && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;

        // Only global admin can update, or delete someone else's, username
        allow update, delete: if isGlobalAdmin();
      }

      // Group registration tokens
      match /registrationTokens/{token} {
        // Allow token validation for everyone (needed for registration)
        allow get: if true;

        // Only group admins or global admin can list or create tokens
        allow list, create: if isGroupAdmin(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();

        // No client may mark a token used any more: `redeemInvitation` spends
        // it in the same transaction that grants membership. The rule this
        // replaces never required the token to be unused or unexpired.

        // Group admins and global admin can also update tokens
        allow update: if isGroupAdmin(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();

        // Only group admins or global admin can delete tokens
        allow delete: if isGroupAdmin(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();
      }

      // Uploads whose document write may still be on its way (T084). The
      // client records one before it uploads and deletes it once the document
      // that points at the file is written; the daily sweep spares the file
      // while the entry is under 30 days old, then deletes both. The entry is
      // named after the file and stamped with the server's time, because it
      // is a lease: a client-chosen time could hold a file forever.
      match /pendingUploads/{uploadId} {
        allow create: if isGroupMember(groupId) &&
                       request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['path', 'uid', 'createdAt']) &&
                       request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid &&
                       request.resource.data.createdAt == request.time &&
                       request.resource.data.path is string &&
                       request.resource.data.path.matches('groups/' + groupId + '/.+/' + uploadId);

        // Only its owner removes it. Nobody reads or changes one: the sweep
        // reads them with the Admin SDK.
        allow delete: if isSignedIn() && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;
      }

      // Campaigns and all campaign content.
      //
      // This replaces the previous `match /{collection}/{docId}` wildcard. That
      // wildcard also matched `users`, `usernames` and `registrationTokens`,
      // which is how a plain member could create registration tokens. Matching
      // `campaigns` explicitly means anything else under a group falls through
      // to the final deny rule instead of inheriting content permissions.
      match /campaigns/{campaignId} {
        allow read: if isGroupMember(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();
        allow create: if isGroupMember(groupId) && imageLeased(groupId, 'banner');

        // RELAXED: any group member may edit campaign metadata.
        allow update: if (isGroupMember(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin()) &&
                       imageLeased(groupId, 'banner');

        // Deleting a whole campaign stays admin-only -- it destroys every
        // entity and every member's notes for that campaign. See bug #1403 and
        // the deleteCampaign Cloud Function, which enforces the same check
        // server-side.
        allow delete: if isGroupAdmin(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();

        // Campaign entities: npcs, locations, quests, rumors, chapters,
        // story-progress, saga -- and anything added later.
        match /{entityCollection}/{entityId} {
          allow read: if isGroupMember(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();
          // `image` is an NPC's or a location's picture (T084); no other
          // entity carries one, so the check passes for them.
          allow create: if isGroupMember(groupId) && imageLeased(groupId, 'image');

          // RELAXED: previously `createdBy == uid || isGroupAdmin`, which the
          // UI never enforced, so members hit permission-denied on any entity
          // they had not personally created (bug #1406).
          allow update: if (isGroupMember(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin()) &&
                         imageLeased(groupId, 'image');

          // RELAXED: previously admin-only, which meant a member could not
          // delete even their OWN entity while the UI showed them a Delete
          // button.
          allow delete: if isGroupMember(groupId) || isGlobalAdmin();
        }
      }
    }

    // Default deny rule
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
